This glossary provides a comprehensive list of domain-specific terms, metrics, and concepts found across the sources, grounded in their specific operational or regulatory contexts.A	•	ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control): An access model where rights are granted through policies combining user, application, and usage attributes.	•	AI Agent: An autonomous principal that generates code and makes access decisions without human mediation.	•	AI Extractions: Capabilities for pulling structured data from unstructured documents like PDF reports or invoices.	•	Albus: An AI agent that mines HRIS and usage data to generate and maintain identity policies autonomously.	•	AS 1000: A PCAOB standard focused on the general responsibilities of an auditor when conducting an audit.	•	AS 1105: A PCAOB auditing standard regarding the sufficiency and appropriateness of audit evidence.	•	AS 1105.10A: A principles-based standard requiring auditors to evaluate the reliability of external electronic information provided by a company.	•	AS 1215: A standard establishing requirements for what must be documented and retained in audit engagement files.	•	AS 2201: A PCAOB standard related to auditing internal control over financial reporting.	•	Article 10 (EU AI Act): A regulation setting data governance requirements for training, validation, and testing datasets in high-risk AI.	•	Article 12 (EU AI Act): A mandate requiring high-risk AI systems to maintain logs for at least six months to support traceability.	•	Article 19 (EU AI Act): A regulation defining a six-month minimum floor for certain AI log retention.	•	Article 99 (EU AI Act): A provision that scales penalties for non-compliance to a percentage of worldwide annual turnover.	•	Audit Trail: A chronological record of system activities sufficient to enable the reconstruction of a sequence of events.	•	Authoritative Source: A system of record, such as an HR platform, that serves as the gateway for identity lifecycle events.B – C	•	Birthright Access: Baseline entitlements assigned automatically to an identity based on attributes like role, department, or location.	•	BUILTIN.DF(): A SuiteQL display function that returns text labels instead of internal numerical IDs for list and record fields.	•	C3PAO (Certified Third-Party Assessment Organization): An entity authorized to perform CMMC certification audits.	•	C&A (Completeness and Accuracy): The standard of verifying that audit evidence is whole and represents actual transactions without error.	•	CMMC (Cybersecurity Maturity Model Certification): A DoD program for securing the defense industrial base, currently undergoing reform.	•	Compliance Mode (S3): A retention setting where objects cannot be deleted or overwritten by any user, including the root account.	•	Confidence Score: A statistical signal indicating the likelihood of an AI output's correctness, often rejected as a substitute for reasoning in audits.	•	Content Hash: A SHA-256 hash of a log record's content computed at the moment of decision to detect subsequent tampering.	•	Content-Addressable Pointer: A reference in an audit record derived from the hash of a large artifact stored in a separate object store.	•	Continuous Control Monitoring (CCM): An automated approach to audit readiness that replaces point-in-time snapshots with 24/7 validation.	•	COSO Framework: A de facto standard for internal control involving components like control environment and monitoring activities.	•	CSPM (Cloud Security Posture Management): Continuous scanning of cloud configurations against security benchmarks to detect misconfigurations.D – G	•	Data Lineage: Documentation and visualization of a data asset's complete path from its origin through transformations to its destination.	•	Delta Access Review: A review process that surfaces only the entitlements that have changed since the last audit cycle.	•	DEMS (Digital Evidence Management System): A platform for centralizing and securing the intake and analysis of investigative digital data.	•	Deterministic Evaluation: A checking logic that uses structured rules rather than probabilistic judgments to verify compliance evidence.	•	DocuMine: A tool for searching and surfacing specific exceptions across large sets of documents like SOC reports.	•	Entitlement: A specific permission or access right granted to an identity within an application.	•	Excel Agents: Virtual audit assistants that execute test procedures directly inside spreadsheets to match and reconcile data.	•	Exponential Backoff: A retry strategy where the wait time between API calls increases to handle service throttling.	•	Governance Mode (S3): A retention setting where objects are protected but users with specific permissions can bypass the lock.H – L	•	Hash Chain: A series of audit records where each includes the hash of the prior record to create a verifiable sequence.	•	Identity Store: A unique identifier for a set of users and groups within an AWS IAM Identity Center instance.	•	Identity Visibility: The ability to see, understand, and fix identity risks across all connected applications and directories.	•	IPE (Information Produced by Entity): Data generated by a company and provided to auditors as evidence for testing controls.	•	IUC (Information Used in Controls): The specific data elements or reports that a control relies on to function effectively.	•	ISU (Integration System User): A dedicated "robot" account in Workday created for API-based integrations.	•	ISSG (Integration System Security Group): A security group in Workday to which an ISU is assigned to control data access.	•	ITGC (Information Technology General Controls): Foundational controls ensuring systems are safeguarded from unauthorized access or changes.	•	JML (Joiner-Mover-Leaver): The lifecycle phases of an identity as a person joins, changes roles, or leaves an organization.	•	Legal Hold: A flag on stored objects that prevents deletion regardless of retention settings, usually for litigation purposes.M – N	•	Material Weakness: A deficiency in internal control over financial reporting such that a material misstatement may not be prevented.	•	Metadata Lineage: The versioned history of how tags, ownership, and classifications about data assets change over time.	•	MicroVM: A hardware-enforced isolation boundary where each execution environment runs its own kernel.	•	Model Provenance: The record of a model's version, configuration, and training-data snapshots at the time of a decision.	•	Nereids Planner: The database component that extracts column-level lineage from the analyzed execution plan of write commands.	•	NHI (Non-Human Identity): Principals like service accounts, API keys, and AI agents that require governed access.	•	NTP-Synced UTC: A standard requirement ensuring audit timestamps are synchronized with an authoritative source and recorded in Coordinated Universal Time.O – R	•	OpenLineage: An open-source standard for capturing lineage metadata from running data pipelines.	•	OSCAL (Open Security Controls Assessment Language): A NIST standard for machine-readable security and compliance data.	•	Orphaned Account: An active account belonging to a user who has been offboarded or no longer needs access.	•	Policy-as-Code: Compliance requirements implemented as version-controlled, testable, and enforceable workflow rules.	•	POA&M (Plan of Action and Milestones): A document identifying tasks to be accomplished in remediating security control failures.	•	Pseudo-pagination: A method for chunking large data requests using report parameters because an API does not support native pagination.	•	RaaS (Reporting as a Service): A Workday feature that exposes custom reports as web service API endpoints.	•	ROC (Risk Operations Center): A platform that consolidates detection, response, compliance, and remediation into one data model.S – Z	•	SAST (Static Application Security Testing): Scanning code for vulnerabilities before deployment as a CI/CD control point.	•	Scale-to-Zero: An infrastructure capability where compute resources are paused when idle while maintaining filesystem state.	•	Significant Deficiency: A control deficiency less severe than a material weakness but important enough to merit governance attention.	•	SIEM (Security Information and Event Management): A platform that aggregates events from across an infrastructure for correlation.	•	SOC 2 Type I: An audit that evaluates whether security controls were properly designed at a specific point in time.	•	SOC 2 Type II: An audit evaluating the operational effectiveness of controls over a period of 6 to 12 months.	•	SuiteQL: A NetSuite interface for running SQL-style queries directly against system tables.	•	3PAO (Third-Party Assessment Organization): An independent entity authorized to perform FedRAMP compliance assessments.	•	WORM (Write Once, Read Many): Storage technology that prevents data from being altered or deleted once written.	•	ZDR (Zero Data Retention): A model where no user data or filesystem state is persisted between execution runs.